\title{Approaching but Not Crossed:\\
Pooling Geometry, a Kendall-Metric Crossing Budget,\\
and the Resolution Wall for a Rank-Correlation Dial}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{1 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study the following question, raised by a monitoring experiment but mathematical in substance: when a monitored rank correlation approaches a pre-registered floor without crossing it, what --- if anything --- can be proved? The concrete record is a Spearman rank-correlation ``dial'' evaluated on a ladder of problem sizes, whose reading at size $84$ is $\rho = 0.558$ with bootstrap interval $[0.536, 0.581]$ and per-replicate readings $0.572$, $0.578$, $0.522$, against a pre-registered floor of $0.55$: a margin of $+0.008$, with the interval straddling the floor. We develop six independent analyses, each yielding a general theorem together with its instantiation at the recorded numbers: (i) a convex aggregate is trapped between its components and pooling is $1$-Lipschitz, so a pooled cliff is a component cliff; (ii) an exact replication criterion gives a fourth-replicate threshold of $0.528$, below the lowest reading already observed; (iii) an exact transposition identity for the Spearman displacement sum yields a sharp adjacent-step bound of $12/n(n+1)$ and a crossing budget of $m\,n(n+1)/12$ adjacent transpositions ($11\,188$ at the recorded margin with $n = 4096$), with a second, independent $\ell^1$-accumulation budget $\rho \ge 1 - 24K^2/n(n^2-1)$, and, as by-products, $\rho(\id)=+1$, $\rho(\rev)=-1$ and a sorting lower bound of $n(n+1)/6$; (iv) the ladder rebounds, so any nonincreasing model must absorb an error of at least $0.00795 = \tfrac{159}{160}$ of the margin, while the local least-squares slope is $+0.001225$ per bit; (v) resolving the margin requires at least $2025/256 \approx 7.91$ times the sample, and a point estimate on the wrong side of the bar cannot be rescued by shrinkage; (vi) crossed and uncrossed hypotheses are realisable by predictors of mutual correlation at least $0.9999$, i.e.\ separated by under $0.81^\circ$, and two admissible contractive fades --- one crossing, one not --- both fit the recorded rungs to within the margin. The unifying conclusion: a cliff in a rank correlation is an $\Omega(n^2)$ rearrangement of the underlying ranking, so gradualness is forced by the metric geometry of the symmetric group rather than observed in the data; and at the recorded resolution the crossing question is not decidable within the natural model class.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Spearman rank correlation; Kendall-tau metric; adjacent transpositions; crossing budget; Bhatia--Davis inequality; monotone approximation; resolution analysis; correlation geometry.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The empirical record}

A \emph{dial} is a scalar diagnostic monitored across a ladder of problem sizes. The dial studied here is a Spearman rank correlation between a structural statistic $T$ of a uniformly drawn integer (a trailing-zero / small-prime measure) and a downstream performance rate. It is evaluated at a ladder of bit-lengths:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcccccccc}
\toprule
bit-length $b$ & 44 & 52 & 64 & 72 & 76 & \textbf{84} & 92 & 96\\
$\rho(b)$ & 0.780 & 0.705 & 0.648 & 0.605 & 0.608 & \textbf{0.558} & 0.563 & 0.5739\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

At bit-length $84$ the pooled reading is $\rho = 0.558$, with a bootstrap confidence interval $[0.536, 0.581]$ (half-width $0.0225$) and three independent replicate readings $0.572$, $0.578$, $0.522$. A floor of $0.55$ was fixed in advance: readings below it declare the dial's signal exhausted. Thus the margin is
\[
0.558 - 0.55 = +0.008 > 0 ,
\]
so the dial has not crossed; but the interval straddles the floor ($0.536 < 0.55 < 0.581$), one replicate is already below it, and the half-width exceeds the margin by a factor of $2.8125$. The recorded verdict is \emph{approaching, not crossed}, with the erosion described as \emph{gradual, not a cliff}.

\subsection{The mathematical question}

The prose verdict is not a theorem. This paper asks what can be proved, and identifies six independent axes on which a rigorous statement is available. Two of them (Sections~\ref{sec:pool} and \ref{sec:repl}) concern the aggregation of replicates; one (Section~\ref{sec:budget}) is a combinatorics/statistics bridge giving a lower bound on the number of elementary rank rearrangements required to move a correlation by a given amount; two (Sections~\ref{sec:mono} and \ref{sec:res}) concern the interaction between the margin and the resolution of the measurement and of the model class; one (Section~\ref{sec:indist}) shows that the two hypotheses under test are geometrically almost identical.

\subsection{Notation}

For a finite index set $I$, weights $w : I \to \mathbb{Q}_{\ge 0}$ with $\sum_i w_i = 1$, and values $x : I \to \mathbb{Q}$, write $\langle w, x\rangle = \sum_i w_i x_i$ for the pooled reading. For rankings we use vectors $\sigma : \{0,\dots,n-1\} \to \mathbb{Z}$ with values in $[0,n)$ (\emph{rank-bounded} vectors), and set
\[
D(\sigma) = \sum_{k=0}^{n-1} \bigl(\sigma(k) - k\bigr)^2, \qquad
\rho_n(\sigma) = 1 - \frac{6\,D(\sigma)}{n(n^2-1)} .
\]
We write $\tau_{ij}\sigma$ for the vector obtained from $\sigma$ by exchanging the \emph{values} at positions $i$ and $j$.

\section{Pooling geometry: a pooled cliff is a component cliff}\label{sec:pool}

\begin{lemma}[Convex trapping]\label{lem:trap}
Let $w$ be nonnegative weights summing to $1$ and $x : I \to \mathbb{Q}$. If $x_i \le M$ for all $i$ then $\langle w,x\rangle \le M$; if $m \le x_i$ for all $i$ then $m \le \langle w,x\rangle$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\langle w,x\rangle \le \sum_i w_i M = M\sum_i w_i = M$, using $w_i \ge 0$ termwise; symmetrically for the lower bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[A pooled crossing forces a component crossing]\label{thm:cross-comp}
Let $w$ be nonnegative weights summing to $1$. If $\langle w, x\rangle < F$, then $x_i < F$ for some $i$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Contrapositive of the lower half of Lemma~\ref{lem:trap}: if every $x_i \ge F$ then $\langle w,x\rangle \ge F$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Pooling is $1$-Lipschitz]\label{thm:lip-pool}
Let $w$ be nonnegative weights summing to $1$ and let $x, y : I \to \mathbb{Q}$ satisfy $|x_i - y_i| \le d$ for all $i$. Then $|\langle w,x\rangle - \langle w,y\rangle| \le d$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\langle w,x\rangle - \langle w,y\rangle = \sum_i w_i (x_i - y_i)$, and each $x_i - y_i$ lies in $[-d,d]$; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:trap} to the family $(x_i - y_i)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:lip-pool} is the formal content of ``a pooled cliff is a component cliff'': if the pooled reading drops by $d$ between two rungs, some replicate dropped by at least $d$. Aggregation never manufactures a discontinuity absent from every component. Conversely a pooled non-crossing can coexist with component crossings, as it does here.
\end{remark}

For the recorded data: the pooled value $0.558$ lies inside the replicate range $[0.522, 0.578]$, as Lemma~\ref{lem:trap} requires; exactly one replicate ($0.522$) is below the floor, so the dial survives on a two-of-three vote; and the replicate mean $209/375 = 0.5573\overline{3}$ reproduces the pooled reading to within $0.001$.

\section{Replication fragility}\label{sec:repl}

\begin{theorem}[Exact crossing criterion under replication]\label{thm:repl}
Let $a,b,c$ be three recorded readings and suppose $k$ further replicates each read $v$. The pooled mean of the $3+k$ readings is below a floor $F$ if and only if $a+b+c+kv < (3+k)F$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Multiply $\frac{a+b+c+kv}{3+k} < F$ by the positive quantity $3+k$; the statements are equivalent.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Fourth-replicate threshold]\label{cor:thresh}
With $a,b,c = 0.572, 0.578, 0.522$ and $F = 0.55$, a single further replicate reading $v$ drives the pooled mean below the floor if and only if $v < 0.528$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
$a+b+c = 1.672$ and $4F = 2.2$; Theorem~\ref{thm:repl} with $k=1$ reads $v < 0.528$.
\end{proof}

The recorded minimum replicate is $0.522 < 0.528$: a single further run merely reproducing an already-observed value tips the pool below the floor. A further run at the recorded maximum $0.578$ does not rescue the pool, only stiffens it. The non-crossing is one replication deep.

\subsection{Dispersion of the replicates}

\begin{theorem}[Bhatia--Davis bound]\label{thm:bd}
For a finite family $x : I \to \mathbb{Q}$ with all values in $[m,M]$ and mean $\mu$,
\[
\Var(x) \le (M-\mu)(\mu-m).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Each term of $\sum_i (M - x_i)(x_i - m)$ is nonnegative, so the sum is $\ge 0$. Expanding and dividing by $|I|$ gives $-\mathbb{E}[x^2] + (M+m)\mu - Mm \ge 0$, i.e.\ $\Var(x) = \mathbb{E}[x^2] - \mu^2 \le (M+m)\mu - Mm - \mu^2 = (M-\mu)(\mu-m)$.
\end{proof}

With $m = 0.522$, $M = 0.578$, $\mu = 209/375$ the recorded replicates have
\[
\Var = \frac{709}{1\,125\,000} \approx 6.3022\times 10^{-4},
\qquad
(M-\mu)(\mu-m) = \frac{1643}{2\,250\,000} \approx 7.3022\times 10^{-4},
\]
a ratio of $0.863$: the dispersion is near-maximal for its range. Moreover $\Var > 9\,(\text{margin})^2 = 5.76\times 10^{-4}$, so the replicate standard deviation ($\approx 0.0251$) exceeds three times the margin. The non-crossing lies well inside replicate noise.

\section{A rank-metric crossing budget}\label{sec:budget}

\subsection{The exact transposition identity}

\begin{theorem}[Transposition identity]\label{thm:transp}
Let $\sigma$ be any integer vector, $i \ne j$ with $i,j < n$. Then
\[
D(\tau_{ij}\sigma) - D(\sigma) = 2\,(j-i)\bigl(\sigma(j)-\sigma(i)\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Split the sum defining $D$ over $\{i,j\}$ and its complement. Outside $\{i,j\}$ the summands are unchanged. On $\{i,j\}$,
\[
\bigl[(\sigma(j)-i)^2 + (\sigma(i)-j)^2\bigr] - \bigl[(\sigma(i)-i)^2 + (\sigma(j)-j)^2\bigr] = 2(j-i)(\sigma(j)-\sigma(i))
\]
by direct expansion: the quadratic terms cancel and only the cross terms survive.
\end{proof}

This is an identity, not an estimate: the entire effect of a transposition is concentrated in the two touched coordinates and is bilinear in the position gap and the value gap.

\begin{corollary}[Spearman transposition law]\label{cor:sp-transp}
For $n \ge 2$,
\[
\rho_n(\tau_{ij}\sigma) - \rho_n(\sigma) = -\,\frac{12\,(j-i)\bigl(\sigma(j)-\sigma(i)\bigr)}{n(n^2-1)} .
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Apply $\rho_n = 1 - 6D/(n(n^2-1))$ to Theorem~\ref{thm:transp}; the denominator is positive for $n \ge 2$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The sharp adjacent-step bound}

Adjacent transpositions ($j = i+1$) generate the Kendall-tau metric on rankings.

\begin{theorem}[Adjacent step bound]\label{thm:adj}
Let $n \ge 2$ and let $\sigma$ be rank-bounded. For $i+1 < n$,
\[
\bigl|\rho_n(\tau_{i,i+1}\sigma) - \rho_n(\sigma)\bigr| \le \frac{12}{n(n+1)} .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Corollary~\ref{cor:sp-transp} with $j-i=1$ the change has absolute value $12|\sigma(i+1)-\sigma(i)|/(n(n^2-1))$. Rank-boundedness gives $|\sigma(i+1)-\sigma(i)| \le n-1$, and $n(n^2-1) = n(n-1)(n+1)$, so the quotient is at most $12(n-1)/(n(n-1)(n+1)) = 12/(n(n+1))$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]\label{thm:sharp}
The constant is optimal. Let $\phi_n$ be the rank-bounded vector with $\phi_n(0) = n-1$, $\phi_n(1) = 0$ and $\phi_n(k) = k$ otherwise. Then
\[
\rho_n(\tau_{0,1}\phi_n) - \rho_n(\phi_n) = \frac{12}{n(n+1)} \quad\text{exactly.}
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Corollary~\ref{cor:sp-transp} with $i=0$, $j=1$ and $\phi_n(1)-\phi_n(0) = -(n-1)$ gives $+12(n-1)/(n(n^2-1)) = 12/(n(n+1))$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The Lipschitz law and the crossing budget}

\begin{theorem}[Lipschitz along Kendall chains]\label{thm:chain}
Let $n \ge 2$, $\sigma$ rank-bounded, and let $\ell$ be a list of $K$ adjacent position pairs inside $\{0,\dots,n-1\}$. Writing $\ell\cdot\sigma$ for the result of applying the transpositions in order,
\[
\bigl|\rho_n(\ell\cdot\sigma) - \rho_n(\sigma)\bigr| \le \frac{12K}{n(n+1)} .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $K$. Rank-boundedness is preserved by transposition (the multiset of values is unchanged), so Theorem~\ref{thm:adj} applies at each step; the triangle inequality accumulates the per-step bounds.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Crossing budget]\label{thm:budget}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:chain}, if $\rho_n(\sigma) - \rho_n(\ell\cdot\sigma) \ge m$ then
\[
K \ \ge\ \frac{m\,n(n+1)}{12}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine $m \le |\rho_n(\ell\cdot\sigma) - \rho_n(\sigma)| \le 12K/(n(n+1))$ and rearrange.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The recorded budget]\label{cor:11188}
With $n = 4096$ paired ranks and margin $m = 0.008$, any chain of adjacent transpositions carrying a reading of at least $0.558$ to a reading below $0.55$ has length at least $11\,188$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
$0.008\cdot 4096\cdot 4097/12 = 11187.49\ldots$, and the length is an integer.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
This is the precise content of ``gradual, not a cliff''. Any crossing of the last $0.008$ is an $\Omega(mn^2)$ rearrangement of the ranking, so gradualness is forced by the metric geometry of the symmetric group rather than observed in the data. It also follows that at large $n$ there is no admissible single move crossing a fixed margin, so ``cliff'' cannot be defined intrinsically --- only relative to the spacing of the ladder.
\end{remark}

\subsection{The two ends of the scale, and a sorting lower bound}

\begin{theorem}[Endpoints]\label{thm:ends}
For $n \ge 2$ the identity ranking $\id(k)=k$ has $D=0$ and $\rho_n = +1$; the reversal $\rev(k) = n-1-k$ has $D = n(n^2-1)/3$ and $\rho_n = -1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$D(\id)=0$ is immediate. For the reversal, $\rev(k)-k = (n-1)-2k$, so $D = \sum_{k<n}((n-1)-2k)^2$; the closed form
\[
3\sum_{k<n}(c-2k)^2 = n\bigl(3c^2 - 6c(n-1) + 2(n-1)(2n-1)\bigr),
\]
proved by induction on $n$, gives $3D = n(n^2-1)$ at $c = n-1$. Substituting into $\rho_n$ yields $-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Sorting lower bound from a rank statistic]\label{cor:sort}
Reversing a list of $n \ge 2$ items requires at least $n(n+1)/6$ adjacent transpositions.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The reversal moves $\rho_n$ from $+1$ to $-1$, a drop of $2$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:budget} with $m=2$.
\end{proof}

This recovers the classical $\Theta(n^2)$ bound by a route that never counts inversions: it comes from the modulus of continuity of a correlation coefficient.

\subsection{A second, independent budget: $\ell^1$ accumulation}

Theorem~\ref{thm:budget} is driven by the worst-case single step, one displacing a rank by $n-1$ positions; such steps cannot be sustained. Write $A(\sigma) = \sum_{k<n}|\sigma(k)-k|$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:l1step}
An adjacent transposition changes $A$ by at most $2$: $A(\tau_{i,i+1}\sigma) \le A(\sigma) + 2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Only the two touched positions contribute, and by the triangle inequality $|\sigma(i+1)-i| + |\sigma(i)-(i+1)| \le |\sigma(i)-i| + |\sigma(i+1)-(i+1)| + 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:sqabs}
$D(\sigma) \le A(\sigma)^2$ for every $\sigma$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\sum_k a_k^2 \le (\sum_k |a_k|)^2$ for real $a_k$: the right side expands to the left side plus nonnegative cross terms.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Quadratic crossing budget]\label{thm:quad}
Let $n \ge 2$. Any ranking reachable from the identity by a chain of $K$ adjacent transpositions satisfies
\[
\rho_n \ \ge\ 1 - \frac{24K^2}{n(n^2-1)} .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$A(\id)=0$, so Lemma~\ref{lem:l1step} and induction give $A \le 2K$; Lemma~\ref{lem:sqabs} gives $D \le 4K^2$; substituting into $\rho_n = 1 - 6D/(n(n^2-1))$ yields the bound.
\end{proof}

At $n = 4096$ and target reading $0.558$ (a drop of $0.442$ from perfect alignment), Theorem~\ref{thm:quad} requires $K \ge 35\,576$ while Theorem~\ref{thm:budget} requires $K \ge 618\,112$: the linear budget dominates for a drop this large. The two scale differently: for a drop $\varepsilon$ from perfect alignment, Theorem~\ref{thm:budget} gives $K \gtrsim \varepsilon n^2/12$ while Theorem~\ref{thm:quad} gives $K \gtrsim \sqrt{\varepsilon n^3/24}$, so the accumulation bound is the stronger one exactly when $\varepsilon \lesssim 6/n$, equivalently $K \lesssim n/2$. At $n = 4096$ the crossover sits at $\varepsilon \approx 0.00146$ and $K \approx 2048$: the $\ell^1$ bound governs the small-margin regime, the single-step bound the large-drop regime. For perspective, the margin to the floor is worth a further $11\,188$ swaps, i.e.\ $11\,188/618\,112 \approx 1.81\%$ of the erosion distance already covered; on the full Spearman scale of width $2$, the margin $0.008$ is $1/250 = 0.4\%$.

\section{Non-monotonicity and the monotone noise floor}\label{sec:mono}

\begin{observation}[Rebound]\label{obs:rebound}
$\rho(84) = 0.558 < \rho(92) = 0.563 < \rho(96) = 0.5739$.
\end{observation}

\begin{theorem}[Monotone-fit noise floor]\label{thm:noise}
Let $d$ be data and $f$ a nonincreasing model with $|f(x)-d(x)| \le \eta$ for all $x$. If $i < j$ and $d(j) > d(i)$, then $\eta \ge \bigl(d(j)-d(i)\bigr)/2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$f(j) \le f(i)$ by monotonicity, while $f(i) \le d(i)+\eta$ and $d(j)-\eta \le f(j)$. Chaining, $d(j)-\eta \le d(i)+\eta$, hence $2\eta \ge d(j)-d(i)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The recorded noise floor]\label{cor:noise}
Any nonincreasing model of the ladder has sup-error
\[
\eta \ \ge\ \frac{0.5739-0.558}{2} = \frac{159}{20\,000} = 0.00795 = \frac{159}{160}\times 0.008 .
\]
\end{corollary}

The unavoidable residual of a monotone fade is $99.375\%$ of the entire margin: within the model class in which ``the dial is approaching its floor'' is even meaningful, the margin is smaller than the model's own noise.

\subsection{The local trend points away from the floor}

For $x_1 < x_2 < x_3$ and ordinates $y_i$, the ordinary least-squares slope is $\hat\beta = \sum_i (x_i-\bar x)(y_i-\bar y) \big/ \sum_i (x_i-\bar x)^2$.

\begin{theorem}[Chebyshev-type positivity]\label{thm:slope}
If $x_1 < x_2 < x_3$ and $y_1 \le y_2 \le y_3$ with at least one strict inequality, then $\hat\beta > 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Use the pair identity $3\sum_i (x_i-\bar x)(y_i-\bar y) = \sum_{i<j}(x_i-x_j)(y_i-y_j)$. Each summand on the right is a product of two nonpositive factors, hence nonnegative, and at least one is strictly positive; the denominator is positive since the $x_i$ are distinct.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Recorded local slope]\label{cor:slope}
Fitting $(84,0.558)$, $(92,0.563)$, $(96,0.5739)$ gives $\hat\beta = 49/40\,000 = +0.001225$ per bit, and extrapolating one rung to bit-length $100$ predicts $0.5739 + 4\hat\beta = 0.5788 > 0.55$.
\end{corollary}

The size-$84$ rung is a local minimum of the recorded ladder: on the recorded evidence the crossing test does not merely fail to fire, the local trend is reversed.

\section{The resolution wall}\label{sec:res}

\begin{theorem}[Resolution cost]\label{thm:rescost}
Let $c > 0$ and suppose the half-width at sample size $m_0 > 0$ is $h_0 = c/\sqrt{m_0}$. If a sample size $m > 0$ achieves half-width at most a target $\mrg > 0$, then
\[
m \ \ge\ \Bigl(\frac{h_0}{\mrg}\Bigr)^{2} m_0 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
From $c/\sqrt m \le \mrg$ we get $c \le \mrg\sqrt m$, and $c = h_0\sqrt{m_0}$, so $(h_0/\mrg)\sqrt{m_0} \le \sqrt m$; squaring (both sides nonnegative) gives the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Recorded resolution factor]\label{cor:791}
With $h_0 = 0.0225$ and target $\mrg = 0.008$,
\[
m \ \ge\ \Bigl(\frac{0.0225}{0.008}\Bigr)^{2} m_0 = \frac{2025}{256}\,m_0 \approx 7.91\,m_0 .
\]
\end{corollary}

Equivalently the recorded half-width exceeds the margin by a factor of $2.8125$, which is exactly why the interval straddles the floor.

\begin{theorem}[Shrinkage cannot move a point estimate]\label{thm:wrongside}
If the centre $c$ of a symmetric interval satisfies $c \le F$, then for every half-width $w > 0$ the lower endpoint satisfies $c - w < F$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$c - w < c \le F$.
\end{proof}

Decisiveness is therefore not always a sample-size problem: Corollary~\ref{cor:791} prices the achievable improvement, Theorem~\ref{thm:wrongside} marks its boundary.

\section{Crossing dichotomy and geometric indistinguishability}\label{sec:indist}

\subsection{Eventual crossing is a statement about the limit}

Model the ladder by a contractive fade $\rho_j = L + a\lambda^{j}$ with $a > 0$ and $0 \le \lambda < 1$.

\begin{theorem}[Crossing dichotomy]\label{thm:dich}
Let $F$ be the band floor, $a > 0$, $0 \le \lambda < 1$. The trajectory $(\rho_j)$ eventually falls below $F$ if and only if $L < F$. Precisely: (i) if $L \ge F$ then $\rho_j \ge F$ for every $j$ and every $a \ge 0$, $\lambda \ge 0$; (ii) if $L < F$ and $\lambda < 1$, then $\rho_j < F$ for all sufficiently large $j$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
(i) $a\lambda^j \ge 0$, so $\rho_j \ge L \ge F$. (ii) $\lambda^j \to 0$ geometrically, so $a\lambda^j < F - L$ for large $j$, whence $\rho_j < F$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The recorded rungs do not pin the limit}

\begin{theorem}[Undecidability at the recorded resolution]\label{thm:undec}
There exist two contractive fades, both reproducing the recorded rungs at bit-lengths $84$, $92$, $96$ to within the recorded margin $0.008$, such that one never falls below the floor $0.55$ and the other eventually does:
\[
\text{Model A: } L = 0.5659,\ a = 10^{-6},\ \lambda = 0.5;
\qquad
\text{Model B: } L = 0.549,\ a = 0.017,\ \lambda = 0.998 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Model A has $L = 0.5659 > 0.55$, so by Theorem~\ref{thm:dich}(i) it never crosses; its first three values are $0.565901$, $0.5659005$, $0.56590025$, each within $0.008$ of $0.558$, $0.563$, $0.5739$ respectively (the largest discrepancy, $0.0080$, occurs at the third rung). Model B has $L = 0.549 < 0.55$ and $\lambda = 0.998 < 1$, so by Theorem~\ref{thm:dich}(ii) it crosses eventually; its first three values are $0.566$, $0.565966$, $0.565932$, again each within $0.008$ of the recorded rungs.
\end{proof}

At its own resolution the ladder is consistent with a dial that dies and with a dial that does not. This is the sharpest available formalisation of ``gradual, not a cliff'': a cliff would separate the two hypotheses; a gradient measured coarser than itself does not.

\subsection{Crossed and uncrossed predictors are $0.9999$-aligned}

For nonzero centred vectors, $\corr(u,v) = \langle u,v\rangle/\|u\|\|v\|$ is the cosine of an angle, and the maximum attainable alignment of two vectors with prescribed correlations against a common $w$ is $\corr(u,w)\corr(v,w) + \sqrt{(1-\corr(u,w)^2)(1-\corr(v,w)^2)}$, realised in a common plane on the same side of $w$.

\begin{theorem}[Indistinguishability of the two hypotheses]\label{thm:geo}
There exist nonzero vectors $u,v,w$ in the plane with
\[
\corr(u,w) = 0.558, \qquad \corr(v,w) = 0.55, \qquad \corr(u,v) \ge 0.9999 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Place $w$ along a fixed axis and take $u,v$ at angles $\theta_u = \arccos(0.558)$ and $\theta_v = \arccos(0.55)$ on the same side. Then
\[
\corr(u,v) = \cos(\theta_u - \theta_v) = 0.558\cdot 0.55 + \sqrt{(1-0.558^2)(1-0.55^2)}
= 0.3069 + 0.693054 > 0.9999 .
\]
The bound is non-vacuous: $0.55 < 0.558$, and the gap between the two target correlations is exactly the recorded margin $0.008$.
\end{proof}

The extremal configuration in fact achieves $\corr(u,v) = 0.99995\ldots$, a separation of $\arccos(0.99995) \approx 0.55^{\circ}$, inside the certified bound $\arccos(0.9999) \approx 0.81^{\circ}$. The crossed and uncrossed hypotheses are therefore realisable by predictors separated by barely half a degree: ``approaching but not crossed'' is a statement about a $10^{-4}$-scale geometric perturbation of the predictor.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Algorithm 1 (Crossing-budget evaluation).}
Input: $n \ge 2$, margin $m > 0$. Compute $B \leftarrow m\,n(n+1)/12$ in exact rational arithmetic and return $\lceil B\rceil$. Cost $O(1)$; correctness by Theorem~\ref{thm:budget}.

\paragraph{Algorithm 2 (Kendall-descent simulation).}
Input: $n$, target reading $\rho^\star$. Maintain the displacement sum $D$ incrementally: a swap at position $i$ updates $D$ by exactly $2(\sigma(i+1)-\sigma(i))$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:transp} with $j-i=1$), so the sum is never recomputed. Greedily perform the swap maximising the increase of $D$ until $\rho \le \rho^\star$, and verify the resulting length against both budgets. Cost $O(1)$ per update, $O(n)$ per greedy selection, $O(nK)$ in total.

\paragraph{Algorithm 3 (Resolution planning).}
Input: recorded half-width $h_0$ at sample size $m_0$, target margin. Return $\lceil (h_0/\mrg)^2 m_0\rceil$; if the point estimate is not strictly above the bar, additionally report that no sample size suffices (Theorem~\ref{thm:wrongside}). Cost $O(1)$; correctness by Theorem~\ref{thm:rescost}.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the six analyses say together}

Read as a portfolio, the results disagree, and that is the finding. \emph{Pessimistic:} one of three replicates is already below the floor; one further replicate at an already-observed value tips the pool (Corollary~\ref{cor:thresh}); the replicate standard deviation exceeds three times the margin. \emph{Optimistic:} the ladder rebounds (Observation~\ref{obs:rebound}) and the local slope points away from the floor (Corollary~\ref{cor:slope}). \emph{Agnostic:} the margin is smaller than the residual of any monotone model (Corollary~\ref{cor:noise}); resolving it needs $\approx 7.91\times$ the data (Corollary~\ref{cor:791}); two admissible fades, one crossing and one not, both fit the rungs to within the margin (Theorem~\ref{thm:undec}). The correct summary is not a verdict but a diagnosis: at the recorded resolution the crossing question is not well posed.

\subsection{What is genuinely transferable}

Section~\ref{sec:budget} turns Spearman's coefficient into a $12/(n(n+1))$-Lipschitz function on the Cayley graph of the symmetric group generated by adjacent transpositions. Three consequences do not depend on the experiment at hand: cliffs in rank correlation are $\Omega(n^2)$ events; ``cliff'' is not intrinsically definable, only relative to a measurement grid; and a correlation coefficient yields a sorting lower bound (Corollary~\ref{cor:sort}). The failures are equally informative. A monotone-fade reading of the ladder is false, not merely difficult, because the data rebounds; and a Gram-geometry certificate separating crossed from uncrossed states stalls because the two realisations are $0.9999$-aligned.

\subsection{Scope and limitations}

The transposition analysis assumes untied ranks and treats $n$ as the number of paired ranks; ties would require the tie-corrected coefficient and perturb the denominators. The resolution analysis assumes the $c/\sqrt m$ half-width law, which is asymptotic. The fade model is a modelling choice: Theorem~\ref{thm:dich} characterises crossing within that class and Theorem~\ref{thm:undec} shows the class is underdetermined by the recorded rungs, but neither statement constrains models outside it.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{Kendall--Spearman budget duality.} Theorem~\ref{thm:budget} is linear in the margin and quadratic in $n$; Theorem~\ref{thm:quad} is quadratic in the swap count and cubic in $n$. Neither is known to be tight. They arise from different conserved quantities --- the size of a single step versus the accumulation of $\ell^1$ displacement --- so their crossover, at a drop of order $6/n$ and a swap count of order $n/2$, should mark a genuine change of extremal geometry on the symmetric group.

\paragraph{An intrinsic cliff criterion for rank dials.} ``Cliff versus gradual'' currently depends on an arbitrary grid. A grid-free criterion can be phrased through the crossing budget: call a ladder cliff-like at a rung if the observed rung-to-rung drop demands a rearrangement superlinear in the change of problem size.

\paragraph{Sharper aggregation geometry.} Theorem~\ref{thm:lip-pool} uses only the sup-metric. Combining Bhatia--Davis (Theorem~\ref{thm:bd}) with the pooling map should give a two-sided prediction interval for the pooled reading in terms of the observed replicate range, upgrading Corollary~\ref{cor:thresh} from a threshold to a probability.

\paragraph{Adaptive resolution planning.} In monitoring practice the margin moves with each rung, suggesting a sequential design problem: allocate sample size across rungs so as to minimise the expected time until the crossing question becomes decidable, subject to the hard obstruction of Theorem~\ref{thm:wrongside}.

\paragraph{Beyond planar realisations.} Theorem~\ref{thm:geo} exhibits an extremal planar configuration. Which correlation matrices are realisable with prescribed pairwise entries, and how the feasible set degenerates as two prescribed correlations approach each other, remains the most promising path to a decisive certificate.

\section{Conclusion}

A monitored rank correlation reading $0.558$ against a floor of $0.55$ raised the question of whether a line had been crossed. The answer is that the question, as posed, is finer than the instrument. The aggregate survives on a two-of-three vote, one replication deep; any crossing of the remaining margin is an $\Omega(n^2)$ rearrangement of the underlying ranking, so cliffs are structurally unavailable; the ladder rebounds, making any monotone fade carry a residual $99.4\%$ the size of the margin; nearly eight times the data would be needed to resolve it; and the crossed and uncrossed hypotheses can be realised by predictors separated by less than a degree. The permanent contribution is the rank-metric machinery: an exact transposition identity, a sharp adjacent-step bound of $12/(n(n+1))$, a Lipschitz law for the Kendall-tau metric, two complementary crossing budgets, and a sorting lower bound derived from a correlation coefficient. Together they say that a rank correlation cannot fall off a cliff: gradualness is not a property of this dataset, but of rankings.

